speechtext:
No, it is not a new
power. It is a power that was provided in 1950 by an item of $1 in the estimates which authorized the federal district commission to enter into and carry out a contract with Canadian National Railways to build these railway facilities. It is to be found in the statutes of 1950, and is schedule C to the Appropriation Act, vote No. 725. It is quite long, but it reads as follows:
To authorize entry into and performance of an agreement, including payment of expenditures incurred by the federal district commission thereunder out of the national capital fund, between the federal district commission and the Canadian National Hailway Company to provide, on such terms and conditions as may be approved by the governor in council, that
(a)	the federal district commission will acquire from His Majesty lands now held by His Majesty for the purpose of a railway cutoff line and freight yard in south Ottawa in accordance with the master plan for the national capital district and will construct, at its expense, the requisite railway facilities thereon;
(b)	the federal district commission will grant a right of user in perpetuity over the new facilities to the Canadian National Railway Company in exchange for a conveyance free of charge of lands and facilities now used by the railway company for which the new facilities are to be substituted, such right of user not to exclude use by other railways of the facilities as may be agreed by the commission;
(c)	the federal district commission will reimburse the Canadian National Railway Company for costs incurred by the company by reason of the abandonment of existing facilities and transfer of operations to the new facilities;
(d)	the Canadian National Railway Company, together with any other railway using the new facilities, will operate and maintain the new facilities at their own expense;
and for matters incidental thereto . . .
That was a $1 vote in the supplementary estimates of 1950. It was felt that though it sufficed at that time to authorize the contract which was made and signed on March 30, 1950, as we were amending the Federal District Commission Act it would be more regular to have the statutory power granted by this provision in that act rather than in an appropriation bill. If the hon. member will look at the estimates for this year he will find under item 327 on page 43 the words, "Appropriation not required for 1951-52, $1." There it is indicated that the details are on page 336, and turning to that page we find this note:
To authorize the federal district commission to enter into an agreement with the Canadian National Railway Company respecting rearrangement of railway facilities, $1.

Federal District Commission Act
We felt the criticism that has sometimes been made in this house that legislation of an enduring character should not be provided by votes in the estimates had some validity, though there are occasions when it is convenient to do it in that way. Having to bring a bill before parliament to deal with the federal district commission, however, we felt that it would probably meet the views of parliament if we put this legislation in the statutes relating to the commission and dropped it from the estimates.
